# Owner Intent

## Purpose

Keep the author of a change from becoming the authority on its scope. Each
failure below has the same shape: text the author wrote — an issue, a PR body, a
doc line added in the same PR — was treated as a decision, and the module
owner's actual position was never read, so a fix grew into a new shared
mechanism the owner had not asked for.

"Owner" means whoever the repository names for that code or rule: a CODEOWNERS
entry, the author of the owning spec, or the maintainer the triage procedure
assigns.

## When To Apply

- Filing a bug, or a follow-up issue out of a review.
- A fix or feature PR that adds or changes a pattern other code must adopt.
- Deciding whether a question is "settled" — in a cycle check, a `disagree`
  reply, or a fix plan.
- Before every push, review re-request, or status report on an open PR.

## Mandatory Behavior

1. **A bug is behaviour a user can hit, with evidence.** Code that diverges from
   a style or design rule is not a bug until the rule's owner has confirmed the
   rule. If the owning spec lists the point as an open question, ask on the
   owner's PR or thread instead of filing.
2. **A bug issue states the defect, the evidence, and the scope** — nothing
   more. It never prescribes a new shared mechanism; a "suggested fix" may name
   only owners and patterns that already exist. A new mechanism is a design
   proposal for the owner, filed as one.
3. **Keep the assignee the filing procedure picks.** That is the triage owner.
   Do not reassign to yourself unless the owner or the user says so.
4. **The owner decides whether a shared pattern changes, not the PR.** Such a
   change ships in its own dedicated PR the owner agreed to. A consistency rule
   ("change it app-wide, never divergently") constrains where a pattern change
   goes; it never licenses adding a new framework inside a fix.
5. **Only owner or reviewer decisions and merged specs are settled.** The
   author's own issue text, PR body, or doc edits made in the same PR are never
   settled inputs — not to a cycle check, not to a `disagree` reply, not to
   rejecting a reviewer's suggestion.
6. **Author gate.** Before every push, re-request, or status report, re-read the
   PR conversation — reviews, issue comments, inline threads — and the linked
   issue since your last read. An unanswered code-owner or reviewer comment
   stops the work until it is answered.
7. **Check a follow-up's direction before filing it.** Compare it with the
   latest owner and reviewer feedback in the thread. A follow-up must not
   entrench what a reviewer asked to remove.

## Output Contract

- A filed issue: defect, evidence, scope, and the assignee left as triaged.
- A status report on an open PR carries `thread re-read at <UTC time>`, and
  names any owner or reviewer comment still unanswered.
- A list of settled inputs names, for each entry, the owner, reviewer, or merged
  spec it comes from.

## Red Flags — Stop and re-read this rule if you are thinking:

| Rationalization | Why it's wrong |
|---|---|
| "The style guide says X and 16 places don't do X, so that is 16 bugs" | A rule gap is a question for the rule's owner until confirmed. If the owner's spec lists it as open, filing it as a bug decides the owner's question for them |
| "The issue says to do it app-wide, so the fix PR builds the shared mechanism" | The issue is the author's text. A shared mechanism needs the owner's agreement and its own PR |
| "Our issue and PR body already settled this, so the reviewer's suggestion is a cycle" | Your own text cannot settle your own review. Only an owner's or reviewer's position on record can |
| "CI is green and two reviewers approved, so the status is done" | Approvals do not answer an owner comment posted after them. Re-read the thread first |
| "I'll take the issue myself, triage would just slow it down" | Triage is the owner's gate. Bypassing it is how an unconfirmed design ships as a bug fix |

**IRON LAW: THE AUTHOR'S OWN WORDS NEVER SETTLE THE AUTHOR'S OWN SCOPE. ONLY THE OWNER DOES.**
